// Crusaders Mainframe — this boot's identity, the third field of the server's
// idempotency key.
//
// WHY THE COURT BOX NEEDS ONE (round G4's C2, owed and now paid)
// --------------------------------------------------------------
// The API deduplicates events on a PERMANENT unique index. The event ring is
// RAM-only, so no event survives a reboot; `seq` resumes from one checkpoint
// record in flash, written in blocks of 64. Erase or tear that record — which
// `hal::IStore` explicitly allows, handing a torn write back as a value with
// `kOk`, and which `eventq` handles by reading it as ABSENT — and the board
// renumbers from 1. Every event of the next evening is then silently receipted
// as a duplicate and discarded, INCLUDING the `boot` event the firmware queues
// precisely so an operator can see that a board restarted. Two boxes that
// accidentally share a MAC dedup each other the same way.
//
// Under charge-at-booking a discarded activation is the no-show sweep refunding
// and penalising a member who played. So the renumber is a money defect, not a
// telemetry one.
//
// A per-boot value makes the key `(device, boot_id, seq)`: a renumbered board
// collides with nothing, and a genuine retry still carries the same three
// fields and is still deduplicated. `main.cpp` passed a literal 0 with a
// comment saying the fix was owed — and 0 is exactly the wire's "this board has
// no boot identity", which omits the field and gives the server the old
// `(device, seq)` behaviour. Nothing about the box was fixed by that comment.
//
// WHY THE SANITISER IS IN lib/ AND THE DRAW IS NOT
// ------------------------------------------------
// Reaching for the part's RNG is wiring and belongs in the composition root,
// where no host test can run it. The one RULE about the value — that it is
// never zero, because a one-in-four-billion draw would silently put one boot of
// one board back on the defect this closes — is a decision, and decisions live
// where `pio test -e native` can reach them. The gate's equivalent writes the
// same rule inline in its own main.cpp, where nothing tests it.
//
// NOTHING TURNS ON IT BEING UNPREDICTABLE. It is a de-duplication tag, not a
// credential: the requirement is only that two consecutive boots of one board,
// and two boards that happen to share a MAC, do not collide.
//
// No Arduino headers, no heap. Compiles for Xtensa and the host.

